  "prediction": "**Rare Nirvana Photos and Web Preservation Efforts Make Headlines**\n\nIn recent news, rare photographs from Nirvana's first concert in March 1987 have been unearthed and shared by Maggie Poukkula, the 19-year-old daughter of Tony Poukkula, a member of Seattle band Laytem who hosted the fledging outfit's first show in his basement. The photos, which feature Kurt Cobain and Poukkula's father playing together, were discovered tucked away inside her father's biographies of Cobain and Nirvana.\n\nThe photos come on the heels of a deluge of rare Cobain and Nirvana artifacts, many of which were incorporated into Brett Morgen's acclaimed documentary, Montage of Heck. The documentary, which features unprecedented access to Cobain's archives, is set to return to movie theaters starting August 7th.\n\nMeanwhile, the Internet Archive is working to preserve web pages and fix broken links on the web. The organization is using crawls to archive pages as they are created and archive the pages that they refer to, allowing users to access the original content even if the referenced page has been changed or removed. This effort aims to make the web a more reliable and accessible resource for users.\n\nIn related news, Rolling Stone has ranked all 102 Nirvana songs, providing a comprehensive look at the band's complete catalog. The ranking is part of a larger effort to celebrate the band's impact on the music world and their role in defining the 1990s music scene.\n\nOverall, these news stories highlight the enduring legacy of Nirvana and the importance of preserving web content for future generations.",
